  ],
  "text": "Commercial bananas cannot reproduce. Every single one is a cutting from the same plant. Look at a slice. There are no seeds in there, only tiny black specks. A plant with no seeds cannot breed. The only way to make more is to cut a piece off. Which means every Cavendish banana on Earth is genetically the same individual. And a population with no variation has no survivors. The banana before this one was called Gros Michel. People who ate it say it was better. A soil fungus called Panama disease found a way through it in the nineteen fifties. Because they were identical, it never needed a second way in. It took the whole crop. Cavendish replaced it because it resisted that strain. A new strain is moving through it now. Same plant, same trick, same problem. We only changed which clone. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
